<div class="subtitle">
<p><strong>TL;DR</strong>: LaTeX in Org mode now go <strong>whoosh</strong>, all pretty like.  Install the development branch of Org mode to use it.</p>
</div>
<p>After grumbling about the inadequacy of LaTeX previews in Emacs &ndash; and specifically Org mode &ndash; for over a decade, <a href="https://tecosaur.net/">Tecosaur</a> and I decided to take the leap and rewrite the feature from the ground up.  The result is a preview system for LaTeX math that can provide near-WYSIWYG feedback but retains the properties we expect in the Emacs environment: text-first, extensible and composable.</p>
<p><span class="org-target" id="org-target--org-latex-preview-youtube-demo"></span></p>
<video preload="metadata" style="center" width="700" controls>
<source src="https://karthinks.com/img/olp-auto-mode-1.mp4" type="video/mp4">
<a href="https://karthinks.com/img/olp-auto-mode-1.mp4">[org-latex-preview video demo 1]</a></video>
<video preload="metadata" style="center" width="700" controls>
<source src="https://karthinks.com/img/olp-auto-mode-inline-3.mp4" type="video/mp4">
<a href=https://karthinks.com/img/olp-auto-mode-inline-1.mp4">[org-latex-preview video demo 3: inline math]</a></video>
<p>It&rsquo;s not much code, but it contains many moving pieces and makes contact with several corners of Emacs.  Now that the feature is part of Org mode, I thought I would take this opportunity to document how it works from the ground up, and use it as a launchpad to explain relevant aspects of Emacs&rsquo; event loop and its unofficial package API
<span class="sidenote-number"><small class="sidenote">
Emacs doesn&rsquo;t really have a &ldquo;package API&rdquo;, since you build Emacs packages from the same elements as Emacs itself.
</small></span>
.</p>
<p>This article is not an exhaustive demo of all the shiny new features, although there are a few videos peppered throughout.  It&rsquo;s also not the user manual (not yet updated on orgmode.org) or a tutorial (see Tecosaur&rsquo;s <a href="https://blog.tecosaur.net/tmio/2026-11-01-preview.html">This Month in Org</a> writeup).</p>
<p>Instead, it aims to provide an overview of the goals and design of the library, along with some explainers of relevant aspects of the Emacs API and tips to write your own features.  This write-up is for you if you are interested in</p>
<ul>
<li>a broad understanding of LaTeX preview generation (how and why),</li>
<li>learning about Emacs&rsquo; event loop, subprocess interaction, overlay-based display and UI, or</li>
<li>a case study on building a non-trivial Emacs &ldquo;application&rdquo; from scratch.</li>
</ul>
