<h2 id="what-is-org-latex-preview">What is <code>org-latex-preview</code>?</h2>
<p>This is a feature included with Org mode to preview LaTeX math snippets as images in Emacs buffers.  The contents of LaTeX fragments and environments in Org mode are replaced with rendered images.</p>
<div class="wide" style="margin-top: 8px">
<div class="column">
  <a href="https://karthinks.com/img/olp-before-1.png"><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/olp-before-1.png" style="width:100%;border-radius:5px;box-shadow: 0px -5px 10px #421D5D"></a>
</div>
<div class="column">
  <a href="https://karthinks.com/img/olp-after-1.png"><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/olp-after-1.png" style="width:100%;border-radius:5px;box-shadow: 0px -5px 10px #421D5D"></a>
</div>
</div>
<p>It has existed for decades at this point, but in a form that I consider barely usable; this rewrite overhauls the code base and adds a number of quality-of-life features.  There is a 13 minute demo that can give you a good idea of the new capabilities of Org mode:</p>
<hr>
<p><span class="org-target" id="org-target--org-latex-preview-everywhere-youtube-demo"></span></p>
<div class="videocontainer"><iframe width="680" height="382" src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/n-AfvuV-bYo" title="YouTube video player" frameborder="0" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" allowfullscreen>[<a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n-AfvuV-bYo">Video Demo (13:13)</a>]</iframe></div>
<hr>
<p>The bullet points, if you prefer:</p>
<ul>
<li>Preview generation is fully asynchronous
<span class="sidenote-number"><small class="sidenote">
Or as asynchronous as Emacs in its current form allows.
</small></span>
, with images placed continuously as they are generated</li>
<li>Bulk generation: Preview 200 to 800 snippets per second</li>
<li>Previews blend in correctly with surrounding colors</li>
<li>Automatically match the current Emacs theme (even through theme changes)</li>
<li>Previews are aligned with and scaled to match the font baseline and size</li>
<li>Previews auto-generate for newly inserted LaTeX</li>
<li>Previews auto-update as you change the underlying LaTeX</li>
<li>Preview as you type: Live-previews of LaTeX fragments</li>
<li>Consistent (correct) equation numbering across the document</li>
<li>LaTeX compilation error reporting for previews</li>
</ul>
<p>And a couple more quick demos:</p>
<p><video preload="metadata" style="center" width="700" controls>
<source src="https://karthinks.com/img/olp-auto-mode-2.mp4" type="video/mp4">
<a href="https://karthinks.com/img/olp-auto-mode-2.mp4">[org-latex-preview video demo 2]</a></video></p>
<video preload="metadata" style="center" width="700" controls>
<source src="https://karthinks.com/img/olp-auto-mode-tikz.mp4" type="video/mp4">
<a href="https://karthinks.com/img/olp-auto-mode-tikz.mp4">[org-latex-preview tikz demo]</a></video>
<p>The rest of this article is mostly about how it works, and briefly why.  As such, it has a &ldquo;fractal&rdquo; level of detail.  There is information intended</p>
<ul>
<li>to give the casual reader a bird&rsquo;s eye view of LaTeX preview generation,</li>
<li>to give the motivated reader a detailed look at its many pieces,</li>
<li>for the potential new Emacs package author looking for pointers on aspects of Emacs&rsquo; API,</li>
<li>for folks wanting to develop a similar feature for applications <em>other</em> than Emacs,</li>
<li>and information that is of no use whatsoever except to the future maintainers of the <code>org-latex-preview</code> library.</li>
</ul>
<p>To that end, many details are tucked away in blocks like the following.  You can safely ignore them in favor of the top-level headings, images and video demos.</p>
<div class="grid">
<details class="details implementation">
<summary><span><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/lisp-icon.png" class=detail-icon></span> Implementation details</summary>
<div class="details">
<p>Blocks like this describe the concrete implementation of aspects of the code.</p>
<p>This is probably only interesting if you are looking to dive into the code itself, or if you are looking to write a similar package.</p>
</div>
</details>
<details class="details apinote">
<summary><span><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/emacs-icon.png" class=detail-icon></span> Emacs API notes</summary>
<div class="details">
<p>Blocks like this are for additional notes on aspects of Emacs&rsquo; API, including the odd code snippets.</p>
<p>Warning: Here be idiosyncratic takes.</p>
</div>
</details>
<details class="details orgnote">
