<h2 id="isn-t-this-a-narrow-feature-to-spend-so-much-time-on">Isn&rsquo;t this a narrow feature to spend so much time on?</h2>
<p>Indeed, this feature</p>
<ul>
<li>requires a LaTeX distribution installed,</li>
<li>works only with Org mode (for now),</li>
<li>and is glued together from several processes and grossly inefficient in the larger scheme of things.</li>
<li>It&rsquo;s also finicky, as all hyper-configurable software is.</li>
</ul>
<p>LaTeX was not built with the aim of minimizing latency.  Having to start multiple new processes even for a one-character change places a hard cap on what&rsquo;s possible in terms of real-time feedback.  Implementing an equivalent preview feature for a system designed for modern computers, like Typst, is trivial in comparison.</p>
<p>Moreover, as popular as Org mode is, it&rsquo;s a small player in the space of markup languages.  The fraction of Org and LaTeX users who would prefer to use Org mode to write LaTeX is smaller still.  And while Org attempts to integrate well with LaTeX, navigating the mishmash of Org and LaTeX syntax presents an extra learning curve on top of learning to use the two markup languages individually.</p>
<p>But it also has a few things going for it.</p>
<p>First, LaTeX is ubiquitous, and Org mode is a far more convenient format for writing than raw LaTeX.  There is significantly less visual noise (think Markdown vs HTML), and Org provides an escape hatch for adding LaTeX verbatim.  Org&rsquo;s simple interface for shuffling text around, monitoring task progress and executing code blocks makes it easy for eventual LaTeX documents to begin as Org files.  Since everything in Emacs composes with everything else, your system for (say) <a href="https://karthinks.com/software/latex-input-for-impatient-scholars/">fast LaTeX input</a> transfers over to Org mode seamlessly.</p>
<p>Second, the fact that Org is versatile as a publishing tool implies that some benefits of better LaTeX support transfer over to other formats too, like equations in HTML or ODT exports.</p>
<p>There is a wide swath of applications built on Org mode that benefit from LaTeX math input, such as Org Roam and Org Noter.  For these applications converting Org documents to LaTeX is not the goal, as they are not primarily publication tools: they stick around as private digital archives, and better LaTeX support is a bonus to the user&rsquo;s expressivity.</p>
<p>Real-time feedback when editing LaTeX snippets is a categorical improvement to the user&rsquo;s experience.  I did not realize this when I started work on <code>org-latex-preview</code>, but now I wouldn&rsquo;t want to LaTeX without it.
<span class="sidenote-number"><small class="sidenote">
It baffles me that web LaTeX editors (Overleaf etc) don&rsquo;t provide this out of the box, especially as they target novice LaTeX users.
</small></span>
.</p>
<p>Finally, we <em>can</em> keep Org mode out of the picture: <code>org-latex-preview</code> can technically work anywhere in Emacs, and in every major-mode. it&rsquo;s just not there yet
<span class="sidenote-number"><small class="sidenote">
(See <a href="#latex-previews-everywhere-in-emacs">below</a>)
</small></span>
.</p>
<p>But perhaps the most germane reason is that Emacs users don&rsquo;t shy away from settling comfortably into tiny, cramped and poorly insulated niches.  Comes with the territory!</p>
<h2 id="the-objectives">The Objectives</h2>
<h3 id="never-block-emacs">Never Block Emacs</h3>
<p>Emacs users are closely acquainted with its frequent unresponsiveness.  You&rsquo;re never more than one minor-mode or mode line indicator away from causing Emacs to block or lag.  While it&rsquo;s not a big ask to wait a second for the result of an action <em>you</em> initiate, the unresponsiveness often manifests as short random freezes, which is unacceptable.  But there&rsquo;s more.  Beyond the annoyance and inconvenience, poor responsiveness denies us an entire class of uses: real-time feedback is everything for interactive applications.</p>
<p>Owing to both its design and age, Emacs is not very well suited for asynchronous applications.  Certain patterns of asynchronous usage are viable (see below), but concurrency support is limited and parallelism is absent: no worker threads, channels, async-io, or anything of the like.</p>
<details class="details apinote">
<summary>Generator-based async in Emacs lisp <span><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/emacs-icon.png" class=detail-icon></span></summary>
<div class="details">
<p>I lied.  In addition to Elisp threads, there exist prototype libraries for async-io (<a href="https://nullprogram.com/blog/2019/03/10/">Chris Wellons</a>) and channels/CSP (<a href="https://andreyorst.gitlab.io/posts/2023-02-01-ad-hoc-async-in-emacs-lisp-via-generators/">Andrey Listopadov</a>) based on Emacs&rsquo; <a href="https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/elisp/Generators.html">generators library</a>, which in turn is implemented using continuation passing.  But generators as provided by this library do not have anywhere near the level of robustness or support you need to build actual applications: no introspection, error handling or debugging support, poor performance, and just overall jankiness.  I&rsquo;ve tried and lived to regret it &ndash; <a href="https://github.com/karthink/elfeed-tube">Elfeed Tube</a> is a real mess.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://elpa.gnu.org/packages/futur.html">Futur</a> package is another promising swing at an async-io style interface, but it&rsquo;s new and I haven&rsquo;t been able to try it yet.</p>
<p>In any event, a more responsive <code>org-latex-preview</code> would require parallelism and not just concurrency.  There is John Wiegley&rsquo;s <a href="https://github.com/jwiegley/emacs-async">async library</a> (and packages inspired by it, like <a href="https://codeberg.org/FelipeLema/session-async.el">session-async</a>) that spawn a child Emacs that runs in parallel, but communication is limited and the overhead is sizeable, so this is a bit of a bludgeon.</p>
</div>
</details>
<p>The upshot is this: we want <code>org-latex-preview</code> to be as asynchronous as possible within Emacs&rsquo; effectively single-threaded event loop model.  The goal is to minimize how long the user has to wait, in terms of both the overall run time and the fraction of that for which Emacs is unresponsive.  We end up having to trade these off against each other.</p>
<h3 id="go-fast--relative-to-latex-and-emacs">Go Fast (relative to LaTeX and Emacs)</h3>
<p>A close second concern is preview generation speed.  This is desirable both in the large and the small.  Previewing upwards of a hundred LaTeX math fragments in a math-heavy Org document used to be an exercise in patience.  Now it takes about one second, and can be much faster with some trade-offs.</p>
