<h3 id="windfalls-consistent-numbering-error-reporting">Windfalls: Consistent numbering, error reporting</h3>
<p>Finally, there are a few features we picked up along the way because they came free.</p>
<p>Recording compilation errors was a simple matter since we scan the LaTeX output anyway.</p>
<p>Consistent equation numbering was expensive in the past because numbering changes can propagate across the document, and parsing the Org document and LaTeX fragment compilation were both slow.  Recent changes to Org have made the former a non-issue (see the <a href="#parsing">parsing section below</a>), and the <a href="#figure--timing-chart-svg-only">performance scaling</a> of our bulk LaTeX runs has fixed the latter.</p>
<p>There are several more minor features along for the ride, such as improvements to editing LaTeX with <code>org-edit-special</code> (<code>C-c '</code>).</p>
<h2 id="the-emacs-api-an-overview">The Emacs API: An Overview</h2>
<p>As mentioned in the introduction, the LaTeX preview system draws on a few different facets of the Emacs API: mainly async process interaction (the &ldquo;backend&rdquo;) and overlays (the UI or &ldquo;frontend&rdquo;).  The live-updating behavior leans heavily on hooks and timers, and we join these pieces together with very simple Lispy data structures, like alists and plists.</p>
<p>We also (naturally) use a few Org mode specific APIs, including ones developed by us for this purpose.  We cover these further below as we encounter them.</p>
<h3 id="the-substrate-emacs-event-loop">The Substrate: Emacs&rsquo; Event Loop</h3>
<p>If you are designing an Emacs application that interacts with the outside world, it helps to start with a mental picture of Emacs&rsquo; event loop.  To a first approximation, the main loop does only four things:</p>
<p><a id="figure--emacs-event-loop-simple"></a></p>
<figure><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/emacs-event-loop-simple.png"/>
</figure>

<ul>
<li>Read input events: This includes keyboard, mouse and touchscreen events, but also some OS signals.  This is the user&rsquo;s interface to Emacs.</li>
<li>Evaluate commands and redisplay: This is the heart of the Lisp evaluator, and Emacs&rsquo; interface to the user.</li>
</ul>
<p>Together these two connect the user and Emacs.  If the main loop did only these two things Emacs would still qualify for the title of &ldquo;Lisp evaluator masquerading as a text editor&rdquo;, but it wouldn&rsquo;t be nearly as useful.  For that we need it to</p>
<ul>
<li>Read subprocess output: This includes output from &ldquo;regular&rdquo; processes like shell commands or LaTeX, but also network requests. This connects Emacs to the world
<span class="sidenote-number"><small class="sidenote">
(your computer and the network)
</small></span>
, and vice-versa.  Command shells, LaTeX previews, LLM clients and most IDE features work because of this.</li>
</ul>
<p>The last event loop function combines the above three, but across time:</p>
<ul>
<li>Run timers: Schedule code for the future and run them when timers are ripe.  Somewhat tentatively, I&rsquo;m including garbage collection here.</li>
</ul>
<p>This design explains why timers in Emacs are not reliable in the absolute sense.  The timer runs not when it&rsquo;s ripe, but when it&rsquo;s ripe <em>and</em> when the main loop gets around to it.  Nevertheless, scheduling code provides a much-needed degree of flexibility to a rigidly ordered task list.</p>
<details>
<summary><span><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/emacs-icon.png" class=detail-icon></span> &ldquo;To a first approximation&rdquo;</summary>
<div class="details">
<p>The way these four tasks are actually organized in the main loop code is less pleasing:</p>
<figure><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/emacs-event-loop-detailed.png"/>
</figure>

<p>Also, Emacs is obviously doing things outside of the event loop as well.  Font caches are compacted, Emacs is responding to the compositor and some OS signals, interacting with dynamic modules, and so on.  In the currently experimental MPS branch, some garbage collection activities also occur in the background.   These are invisible to the Lisp evaluator and thus out of scope when learning to build an Elisp package.</p>
</div>
</details>
<h3 id="the-backend-subprocess-interaction">The Backend: Subprocess Interaction</h3>
<p><a id="figure--async-interaction-via-processes"></a></p>
<figure><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/async-interaction-via-processes.png" width="450px"/>
</figure>

<p>This design suggests that <em>Emacs can only do one thing at a time</em>
<span class="sidenote-number"><small class="sidenote">
As of Emacs 31
</small></span>
, so that designing a responsive application is a steep ask.   But the situation is more nuanced:</p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>Yes, the Lisp evaluator holds a global interpreter lock.  At any point in time Emacs is doing exactly one of the following: painting the screen (redisplay), updating syntax highlighting (fontification), running hook functions or timers, responding to window changes, handling keyboard input, and &ndash; crucially for us &ndash; interacting with subprocesses.  Every action is thus <span class="Orange">blocking</span>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>The only <span class="Purple">asynchronous</span> interaction possible is by starting an OS-level subprocess
<span class="sidenote-number"><small class="sidenote">
Another lie, since we have <a href="#org-target--org-latex-preview-dynamic-modules">dynamic modules, covered below</a>.
</small></span>
.  Emacs continues to run independently from the subprocess, but it still has to handle its <code>STDOUT</code> and <code>STDERR</code> streams and respond to process signals.  When it is doing this, it cannot pay attention to your input or anything else.  Again, Emacs can only do one thing at a time.</p>
<details class="details apinote">
<summary><span><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/emacs-icon.png" class=detail-icon></span> Starting asynchronous subprocesses</summary>
<div class="details">
<p>There are multiple commands to start an asynchronous subprocess from Emacs (see <code>M-x shortdoc</code> <code>process</code>), but <code>start-process</code> covers most common use cases.</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#fff;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"><code class="language-emacs-lisp" data-lang="emacs-lisp"><span style="display:flex;"><span>      (<span style="color:#007020">setq</span> <span style="color:#963">my-process-object</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>            (<span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">start-process</span> <span style="background-color:#fff0f0">&#34;name&#34;</span> <span style="background-color:#fff0f0">&#34;buffer-name&#34;</span> <span style="background-color:#fff0f0">&#34;program&#34;</span> <span style="background-color:#fff0f0">&#34;--arg1&#34;</span> <span style="background-color:#fff0f0">&#34;--arg2&#34;</span> <span style="background-color:#fff0f0">&#34;...&#34;</span>))
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Processes are (optionally) associated with a buffer where process output is inserted.  To send a subprocess some input, there&rsquo;s <code>process-send-string</code> and <code>process-send-region</code>.  Responding to process output and signals is the responsibility of process filters and sentinels (<a href="#org-target--org-latex-preview-process-filters-sentinels">next block</a>).  <code>kill-process</code> does what you&rsquo;d expect.  All of these functions take the process object that <code>start-process</code> returns as an argument.</p>
<p>Except for some <a href="#org-target--org-latex-preview-fiddly-details">fiddly details</a>, that&rsquo;s essentially all there is to the process interaction API.  There are several variants of <code>start-process</code> available &ndash; the synchronous <code>call-process</code>, the shell versions <code>start-process-shell-command</code> and <code>call-process-shell-command</code>, and so on.  The only one that does something different is the primitive <code>make-process</code>, which is a more extensive version that can (additionally) create network processes, send data through TCP or Unix sockets and so on.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<p>Emacs will try to prioritize user input events.  To do this it can delay tasks like reading from subprcesses if necessary.  <a href="https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/elisp/Idle-Timers.html">Idle timers</a> are another example of this.  Some actions are thus <span class="Green">delayable</span>, but they block Emacs when they (eventually) run.  There might be a penalty to pay afterwards, for instance when there&rsquo;s more subprocess output to process as a result of delaying the task.</p>
<p><span class="org-target" id="org-target--org-latex-preview-process-filters-sentinels"></span></p>
<details class="details apinote">
<summary><span><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/emacs-icon.png" class=detail-icon></span> Process filters and sentinels: Handling process output</summary>
<div class="details">
<p>By default, Emacs inserts the output from a subprocess into the process buffer, or ignores it if there is no process buffer.  (Crucially, it still has to read the output from the process pipe.)  This is good enough for simple tasks.  To use or transform subprocess output in more specialized ways, you can write an elisp function that is used as a <em>process filter</em>.  It is called whenever Emacs reads new process output.  In the language of shell commands, I think of process filters like this:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#fff;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"><code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash"><span style="display:flex;"><span>      $ start-process <span style="background-color:#fff0f0">&#34;some-process&#34;</span> 2&gt;&amp;<span style="color:#60e;font-weight:bold">1</span> | process-filter
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Process filters can be tricky to write if you want to parse structured process output, because the amount of data that Emacs reads from the pipe can be arbitrary.  Unless latency is a concern, it&rsquo;s often preferable to work with <em>process sentinels</em> for this reason.  Sentinels are the second tool Emacs makes available for reacting to processes.  A process sentinel is an elisp function (callback) called whenever the process changes state or raises a signal, including when it terminates.  Practically speaking, I think of them like this:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#fff;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"><code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash"><span style="display:flex;"><span>      $ start-process <span style="background-color:#fff0f0">&#34;some-process&#34;</span> 2&gt;&amp;<span style="color:#60e;font-weight:bold">1</span> &gt; process-buffer <span style="color:#333">&amp;&amp;</span> <span style="color:#666;background-color:#fff0f0;font-weight:bold">\
</span></span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span><span style="color:#666;background-color:#fff0f0;font-weight:bold"></span>          process-sentinel &lt; process-buffer
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>They are typically used for cleaning up after the process, and updating Emacs&rsquo; state depending on how the subprocess ends.  But you can use them to access the process buffer containing all the process output, and thus do whatever you would in the filter.</p>
<p>Filters and sentinels can be attached to a subprocess object created with <code>start-process</code> using <code>set-process-filter</code> and <code>set-process-sentinel</code> respectively.</p>
</div>
</details>
</li>
<li>
<p>Many blocking operations can be interrupted by signaling a <code>quit</code>: this is what pressing <code>C-g</code> does, with some variations, in most contexts.  Crucially for us, however, quitting is suppressed when Emacs is handling subprocess output or signals
<span class="sidenote-number"><small class="sidenote">
This is to keep your <code>C-g</code> from doing something unpredictable to &ldquo;background&rdquo; code that you didn&rsquo;t initiate.
</small></span>
.  If Emacs is blocked because it&rsquo;s reading a large data dump&hellip; well, tough luck.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<details class="details apinote">
<summary>Interrupting code <span><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/emacs-icon.png" class=detail-icon></span></summary>
<div class="details">
<p><code>keyboard-quit</code> (<code>C-g</code>) is a <em>specific</em> input that interrupts <em>any</em> code, with a few exceptions.</p>
<p>The obverse of this is to wrap code in a <code>while-no-input</code> clause &ndash; now <em>any</em> input will interrupt the processing of <em>this</em> specific block.  This can be used to do background processing in small chunks without making Emacs unresponsive, but there are subtleties involved that I&rsquo;m not familiar with.</p>
</div>
</details>
<p>Despite these carveouts, a package that interacts heavily with external processes faces a latency-throughput tradeoff.  The more time Emacs spends starting processes and managing their output, the less attention it can pay to your input.  Designing Org&rsquo;s LaTeX preview thus involves finding an acceptable balance.</p>
<p><a id="figure--emacs-latency-vs-throughput"></a></p>
<figure><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/emacs-latency-vs-throughput.png"
         alt="Figure 2: (Top) Emacs dealing with a &amp;ldquo;fat&amp;rdquo; high-throughput process, where it spends a chunk of time reading subprocess output. The time between reading your input and running the resulting command is thus longer than (bottom) the &amp;ldquo;thin&amp;rdquo; low-throughput case."/><figcaption>
            <p><span class="figure-number">Figure 2: </span>(Top) Emacs dealing with a &ldquo;fat&rdquo; high-throughput process, where it spends a chunk of time reading subprocess output.  The time between reading your input and running the resulting command is thus longer than (bottom) the &ldquo;thin&rdquo; low-throughput case.</p>
        </figcaption>
</figure>

<p>It is <em>significantly</em> easier to write a large Emacs library than an equally versatile plugin for most plugin systems built around multi-threaded GUI applications.  I say this as a non-programmer who has only succeeded at the former.  We lean on the interpreter lock in many ways, choosing <a href="#how-it-works-data-flow">simple data structures</a> and <a href="#how-it-works-control-flow">control flow</a> that wouldn&rsquo;t work if Emacs was multi-threaded.  But most of Emacs wouldn&rsquo;t work if Emacs was multi-threaded, anyway.  In for a penny&hellip;</p>
<h3 id="the-frontend-displaying-images-over-text">The Frontend: Displaying images over text</h3>
<p><a href="https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/elisp/Overlays.html">Overlays</a> are a way to alter the appearance or behavior of regions of buffer text without modifying the text itself.  Displaying images is one of their most common uses: LaTeX preview images are overlaid on top of the source fragments.</p>
<figure><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/olp-before-and-after-2.png"/>
</figure>

<p>Once you have the preview images in hand, placing them in overlays and managing their state is essentially all the LaTeX preview code does.</p>
<p>In fact, overlays account for much of the display trickery in Emacs:  Images over text?  Overlays. &ldquo;Virtual text&rdquo; like in <code>whitespace-mode</code>?  Overlays.  <a href="https://github.com/xiongtx/eros">Inline results</a> of code evaluation: overlays. <a href="https://github.com/magnars/multiple-cursors.el">Multiple cursors</a>? Those are overlays.  Emacs has only one cursor.</p>
<details class="details apinote">
<summary><span><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/emacs-icon.png" class=detail-icon></span> Making overlays</summary>
<div class="details">
<p>For a quick look into the overlay API, run <code>M-x shortdoc</code> <code>overlay</code>.</p>
<p>The even shorter version: We can create an overlay <code>ov</code> from buffer position <code>BEG</code> to <code>END</code> with</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#fff;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"><code class="language-emacs-lisp" data-lang="emacs-lisp"><span style="display:flex;"><span>(<span style="color:#007020">setq</span> <span style="color:#963">ov</span> (<span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">make-overlay</span> <span style="color:#963">BEG</span> <span style="color:#963">END</span>))
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>and move it around with</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#fff;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"><code class="language-emacs-lisp" data-lang="emacs-lisp"><span style="display:flex;"><span>(<span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">move-overlay</span> <span style="color:#963">ov</span> <span style="color:#963">NEW-BEG</span> <span style="color:#963">NEW-END</span>)
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>To avoid creating garbage, it is preferable to reuse overlays when possible instead of throwing them away with <code>(delete-overlay ov)</code> and starting over. <code>move-overlay</code> can move them to other buffers too.</p>
<p>The only other tricky question is what happens to the overlay when text is inserted at its left or right boundaries.  Does it expand to contain the text?  This is left as an exercise for the reader.</p>
</div>
</details>
<p>These alterations are specified by adding or modifying the overlay&rsquo;s &ldquo;properties&rdquo;.  Like <a href="https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/elisp/Symbol-Properties.html">symbol properties</a>, overlay properties are a way to store arbitrary information along with the overlay, and <code>org-latex-preview</code> uses them to keep track of state, such as whether the preview image is out of date because the LaTeX fragment has been edited, and to store LaTeX compilation errors for the corresponding fragment.</p>
<details class="details apinote">
<summary><span><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/emacs-icon.png" class=detail-icon></span> Overlay properties</summary>
<div class="details">
<p>To set and retrieve overlay properties, we can use</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#fff;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"><code class="language-emacs-lisp" data-lang="emacs-lisp"><span style="display:flex;"><span>(<span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">overlay-put</span> <span style="color:#963">ov</span> <span style="color:#963">prop</span> <span style="color:#963">value</span>)
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>(<span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">overlay-get</span> <span style="color:#963">ov</span> <span style="color:#963">prop</span>)
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>where the property <code>prop</code> is any elisp symbol.  There is also an <code>overlay-properties</code> function to access the full list.  Once again, <code>M-x shortdoc</code> <code>overlay</code> has us covered.</p>
</div>
</details>
<figure class="alignright"><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/olp-phi-overlaid.png"/>
</figure>

<p>Some properties have special meanings as interpreted by Emacs&rsquo; display engine.  <code>display</code>, the most common property, changes how the overlay region appears on screen, and this is typically set to the image we want to see in place of the text.</p>
<p>But several more interesting modifications are possible with overlays, such as changing the face (font+style) of the underlying text, making it invisible, making it intangible to the cursor, or specifying keymaps that are activated when the cursor is within the bounds of the overlay.</p>
<details class="details apinote">
<summary><span><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/emacs-icon.png" class=detail-icon></span> Fun with overlay properties</summary>
<div class="details">
<p>The only other detail we need is the <a href="https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/elisp/Overlay-Properties.html">list of overlay properties</a> that Emacs considers special.  The <code>display</code> property can be used to show an image instead of the text &ldquo;below&rdquo; (or &ldquo;under&rdquo;) the overlay, for example:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#fff;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"><code class="language-emacs-lisp" data-lang="emacs-lisp"><span style="display:flex;"><span>(<span style="color:#007020">let</span> ((<span style="color:#963">img</span> (<span style="color:#963">create-image</span> <span style="background-color:#fff0f0">&#34;/path/to/image.png&#34;</span>)))
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>  (<span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">overlay-put</span> <span style="color:#963">ov</span> <span style="color:#a60;background-color:#fff0f0">&#39;display</span> <span style="color:#963">img</span>))
</span></span></code></pre></div><hr>
<p>The <code>face</code> property can change the display of the text.  Here we dim the text the overlay covers:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#fff;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"><code class="language-emacs-lisp" data-lang="emacs-lisp"><span style="display:flex;"><span>(<span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">overlay-put</span> <span style="color:#963">ov</span> <span style="color:#a60;background-color:#fff0f0">&#39;face</span> <span style="color:#a60;background-color:#fff0f0">&#39;shadow</span>)
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Here are a few more interesting uses of other special overlay properties.</p>
<hr>
<p>Send a message if the text under the overlay changes:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#fff;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"><code class="language-emacs-lisp" data-lang="emacs-lisp"><span style="display:flex;"><span>(<span style="color:#007020">defun</span> <span style="color:#963">notify-on-ov-change</span> (<span style="color:#963">ov</span> <span style="color:#963">after-p</span> <span style="color:#963">beg</span> <span style="color:#963">end</span> <span style="color:#038;font-weight:bold">&amp;optional</span> <span style="color:#963">_</span>)
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>   (<span style="color:#007020">when</span> <span style="color:#963">after-p</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>     (<span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">message</span> <span style="background-color:#fff0f0">&#34;Text changed: %s&#34;</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>              (<span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">buffer-substring-no-properties</span> <span style="color:#963">beg</span> <span style="color:#963">end</span>))))
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>(<span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">overlay-put</span> <span style="color:#963">ov</span> <span style="color:#a60;background-color:#fff0f0">&#39;modification-hooks</span> (<span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">list</span> <span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">#&#39;</span><span style="color:#963">notify-on-ov-change</span>))
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>You can use this to set up a &ldquo;watcher&rdquo; &ndash; perhaps run a process when a region inside a buffer changes
<span class="sidenote-number"><small class="sidenote">
If you want to set up a watcher on the contents of the entire buffer as opposed to a piece of it, you should avoid overlays and look at the hook <code>after-change-functions</code> instead.  Be warned: it&rsquo;s easy to slow down Emacs to a crawl this way if you&rsquo;re not careful.
</small></span>
!  This is not hypothetical: <code>preview-latex</code> does this to <a href="#precompilation">re-dump the LaTeX preamble</a> of TeX files (in the background) as you edit them.</p>
<hr>
<p>Add a highlight and tooltip (&ldquo;hover text&rdquo;) when the mouse is on the overlay:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#fff;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"><code class="language-emacs-lisp" data-lang="emacs-lisp"><span style="display:flex;"><span>(<span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">overlay-put</span> <span style="color:#963">ov</span> <span style="color:#a60;background-color:#fff0f0">&#39;mouse-face</span> <span style="color:#a60;background-color:#fff0f0">&#39;highlight</span>)
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>(<span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">overlay-put</span> <span style="color:#963">ov</span> <span style="color:#a60;background-color:#fff0f0">&#39;help-echo</span> <span style="background-color:#fff0f0">&#34;You&#39;re on an overlay, pal.&#34;</span>)
</span></span></code></pre></div><hr>
<p>Open the displayed image externally when pressing <code>RET</code> with the cursor on it:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#fff;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"><code class="language-emacs-lisp" data-lang="emacs-lisp"><span style="display:flex;"><span>(<span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">overlay-put</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span> <span style="color:#963">ov</span> <span style="color:#a60;background-color:#fff0f0">&#39;keymap</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span> (<span style="color:#963">define-keymap</span> <span style="background-color:#fff0f0">&#34;RET&#34;</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>   (<span style="color:#007020">lambda</span> () (<span style="color:#007020">interactive</span>)
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>     (<span style="color:#963">when-let*</span> ((<span style="color:#963">img</span> (<span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">get-char-property</span> (<span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">point</span>) <span style="color:#a60;background-color:#fff0f0">&#39;display</span>))
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>                 ((<span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">imagep</span> <span style="color:#963">img</span>))
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>                 (<span style="color:#963">file</span> (<span style="color:#963">image-property</span> <span style="color:#963">img</span> <span style="color:#007020">:file</span>)))
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>       (<span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">start-process</span> <span style="background-color:#fff0f0">&#34;view-image&#34;</span> <span style="color:#036;font-weight:bold">nil</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>                      <span style="background-color:#fff0f0">&#34;xdg-open&#34;</span> <span style="color:#963">file</span>)))))
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>This works by defining a <code>keymap</code> overlay property with the required function.  (On non-Linux systems, you will want to change <code>&quot;xdg-open&quot;</code> to an equivalent command-line utility.)</p>
<hr>
<p>Center an image in a window:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#fff;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"><code class="language-emacs-lisp" data-lang="emacs-lisp"><span style="display:flex;"><span>(<span style="color:#007020">let*</span> ((<span style="color:#963">img</span> (<span style="color:#963">image-create</span> <span style="background-color:#fff0f0">&#34;/path/to/image.png&#34;</span>))
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>       (<span style="color:#963">spacing</span> (<span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">propertize</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>                 <span style="background-color:#fff0f0">&#34; &#34;</span> <span style="color:#a60;background-color:#fff0f0">&#39;face</span> <span style="color:#a60;background-color:#fff0f0">&#39;default</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>                 <span style="color:#a60;background-color:#fff0f0">&#39;display</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>                 <span style="color:#333">`</span>(<span style="color:#963">space</span> <span style="color:#007020">:align-to</span> (<span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">-</span> <span style="color:#963">center</span> (<span style="color:#60e;font-weight:bold">0.5</span> <span style="color:#333">.</span> <span style="color:#333">,</span><span style="color:#963">img</span>))))))
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>  (<span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">overlay-put</span> <span style="color:#963">ov</span> <span style="color:#a60;background-color:#fff0f0">&#39;display</span> <span style="color:#963">img</span>)
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>  (<span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">overlay-put</span> <span style="color:#963">ov</span> <span style="color:#a60;background-color:#fff0f0">&#39;before-string</span> <span style="color:#963">spacing</span>))
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>This image stays centered as you resize the window:</p>
<video preload="metadata" style="center" width="700" controls>
<source src="https://karthinks.com/img/olp-overlay-image-centered.mp4" type="video/mp4">
<a href="https://karthinks.com/img/olp-overlay-image-centered.mp4">[Video: Centered images using overlays]</a></video>
<p>This one needs a little explanation:  The <code>before-string</code> overlay property displays an object before the overlay itself.  The object we choose to display is some propertized text with a <code>display</code> <em>text-property</em>.  The <code>display</code> text-property provides the means to <a href="https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/elisp/Pixel-Specification.html">insert spaces of specified widths</a>, and a symbolic way to refer to the text margins, window center and horizontal window boundary positions.  Combining the two gives us (consistently) centered images in Emacs.</p>
</div>
</details>
<h3 id="the-reactive-ui-hooks-and-timers">The Reactive UI: Hooks and Timers</h3>
<p>In its basic form, the LaTeX preview feature involves no reactive behavior: there is one command to overlay preview images on LaTeX fragments, another to clear those overlays, and nothing else.</p>
<p>But this is a far cry from our goal of real-time feedback.  For LaTeX previews to be automatically generated and updated as we type, we need both timers and <em>hooks</em>.</p>
<p>Hooks are variables provided by Emacs where we can register code to run at certain points.  In this case, we want to react primarily to two kinds of user inputs:</p>
<ul>
<li>Cursor motion, so that entering a previewed LaTeX fragment reveals its source and leaving it re-displays the image.</li>
<li>Buffer changes, so that a preview generation subprocess automatically fires when a new LaTeX fragment is typed in or an existing one is edited.</li>
</ul>
<p>In turn, <code>org-latex-preview</code> exposes hooks of its own for consumers to run additional processing.  These fire when LaTeX subprocesses finish, when overlays are created, and so on.</p>
<p>Timers are complementary to hooks, used to space out or limit the consequences of hooks firing.  They are used</p>
<ul>
<li>to keep these hooks from firing <em>all the time</em>, which would create redundant subprocesses with each edit, locking up the main loop.  They provide the means to implement <a href="https://karthinks.com/software/cool-your-heels-emacs/">throttling and debouncing</a>.</li>
<li>to delay clean-up tasks like deleting intermediate LaTeX output until Emacs is idle.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="the-structure-of-org-latex-preview">The structure of <code>org-latex-preview</code></h2>
<p>Besides basic Elisp data structures, that&rsquo;s everything we need from Emacs to implement non-blocking, fast LaTeX previews.  Assuming you have a handle on what <code>org-latex-preview</code> does, we can start peering into the how.</p>
<h3 id="how-it-works-control-flow">How it works: control flow</h3>
<p>Here is a high-level overview of the control flow of LaTeX preview generation:</p>
<p><a id="figure--org-latex-preview-process-chart"></a></p>
<figure><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/olp-control-flowchart.svg"
         alt="Figure 3: Control flow for Org mode&amp;rsquo;s LaTeX preview system."/><figcaption>
            <p><span class="figure-number">Figure 3: </span>Control flow for Org mode&rsquo;s LaTeX preview system.</p>
        </figcaption>
</figure>

<p>This is a rough picture of how LaTeX previews are generated in Org mode, subdivided into tasks
<span class="sidenote-number"><small class="sidenote">
The made-up term &ldquo;task&rdquo; corresponds to a handful of functions devoted, in our heads, to one purpose.
</small></span>
.  Each action belongs to one of three categories:</p>
<ul>
<li><span class="Orange">Blocking</span> tasks: These must run synchronously and thus require the user to wait while Emacs is unresponsive.  We want this to be minimal and/or done ahead of time.</li>
<li><span class="Green">Delayable</span> tasks: These tasks can be delayed if more immediate actions &ndash; such as accepting user input &ndash; are pending.  However, once such a task is started it runs synchronously and we&rsquo;re locked in.  We want this work to be chunked into small independent pieces.</li>
<li><span class="Purple">Asynchronous</span> tasks: These are subprocesses spawned by Emacs and managed by the OS.  Emacs monitors the process status and handles process output in a delayable fashion, but is otherwise unencumbered by them.</li>
</ul>
<p>When a LaTeX preview command is issued, the first chunk of processing (the <span class="Orange">PREPARE</span> block)</p>
<ol>
<li>sets up overlays in the buffer that &ldquo;cover&rdquo; the fragments, with the intent to add preview images to them at the end,</li>
<li>creates a TeX file containing the relevant LaTeX fragments from the Org buffer, along with a preamble.  This preamble is optionally precompiled, which can speed up processing considerably.</li>
</ol>
<p>It then starts a two step process chain (the <span class="Purple">RUN</span> block) that runs asynchronously.  The end result of a successful run is a preview image or series of images.</p>
<p>The <span class="Green">PROCESS FILTERS</span> block: From this point on until either process ends, Emacs checks in periodically through the associated process filter, acting on the <code>STDOUT</code> to capture various bits of preview metadata.  It also keeps track of images that have finished processing, and (synchronously) caches them and adds them to the overlays in the buffer for these fragments.  This continuous update is doubly useful:</p>
<ul>
<li>Previews start appearing in the buffer much faster, with the window viewport being updated first.</li>
<li>This processing happens in small chunks instead of all at once at the end, so Emacs stays more (even if not fully) responsive to input.</li>
</ul>
<p>The <span class="Orange">POSTPROCESS</span> block: After the subprocesses have finished, Emacs runs their process sentinels, which clean up the detritus of this process chain, or reset the buffer if the preview run failed.</p>
<p>And speaking of detritus&hellip;</p>
<h3 id="how-it-works-file-i-o">How it works: file I/O</h3>
<p><a id="figure--org-latex-preview-file-chart"></a></p>
<figure><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/olp-files-flowchart.svg"/>
</figure>

<p>This figure shows the same process but with an emphasis on disk I/O.  The primary chain is <span style="background: #88c0d0;color:black">TeX file</span> → <span style="background: #88c0d0;color:black">DVI/PDF output file</span> → <span style="background: #88c0d0;color:black">SVG/PNG preview images</span>.</p>
<p>Of the file categories involved in the process, we care about two: the &ldquo;format dump file&rdquo; produced by precompilation, and the preview images.  Both are cached and persisted across Emacs sessions.  All other produced files are deleted by the POSTPROCESS block.</p>
<h3 id="how-it-works-data-flow">How it works: data flow</h3>
<p>Displaying previews that are color-, alignment- and size-aware, along with associated information like errors, requires a considerable amount of bookkeeping.  Since this information arrives piecemeal and processing happens asynchronously in chunks, we also need some way of storing and passing along this context.  There are perhaps clever ways to do this, but we use a straightforward solution &ndash; a (dynamic) global plist that associates each active process chain with its data (see <a href="#org-target--org-latex-preview-org-async-explainer">org-async</a> below).</p>
<figure class="alignright"><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/olp-data-extended-info.svg"/>
</figure>

<p>All of the data generated and required for the many tasks in our process chain are captured in a single mutable container, referred to here &ndash; for convenience &ndash; as &ldquo;Extended Info&rdquo; (<code>extended-info</code>).  This data includes</p>
<ul>
<li>User preferences for the preview run, such as the LaTeX compiler in use,</li>
<li>Input LaTeX strings to be rendered,</li>
<li>Input file paths, output image paths and image cache details,</li>
<li>Emacs&rsquo; context like the current buffer &ndash; which is important since we need to restore it before we can run asynchronous callbacks,</li>
<li>Overlay positions and properties,</li>
<li>Compilation errors,</li>
<li>Computed preview geometry and fontsize for alignment,</li>
</ul>
<p>and more besides.</p>
<details class="details implementation">
<summary><code>extended-info</code>: implementation <span><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/lisp-icon.png" class=detail-icon></span></summary>
<div class="details">
<p>&ldquo;Extended Info&rdquo; is implemented as a plist (property list).  Actually, almost every compound data structure <code>org-latex-preview</code> uses is either an alist or a plist, which is just a linked-list under the hood.  <code>extended-info</code> is also a tree since a few of its members are plists themselves (<a href="#org0ce9534">see below</a>).</p>
<p>We used property lists at first since they&rsquo;re mutable and schema-less, and the design of the library was very fluid.  Unfortunately we are now well past the point where we could switch to something more structured like <code>cl-defstruct</code>, so plists it is.</p>
<p>If it&rsquo;s any consolation, most of Org works with large property lists too.  Org&rsquo;s export system passes around a big <code>plist</code> bucket for communication in much the same way we do here.  Access can get expensive as they grow, but at least passing them around is cheap.  It&rsquo;s&hellip; not much of a consolation.</p>
</div>
</details>
<details class="details apinote">
<summary><code>apply</code> yourself: property lists as arguments <span><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/emacs-icon.png" class=detail-icon></span></summary>
<div class="details">
<p>plists have one other coincidental(?) advantage: You can splice them in as arguments to a <code>cl-defun</code>.</p>
<p>Consider:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#fff;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"><code class="language-emacs-lisp" data-lang="emacs-lisp"><span style="display:flex;"><span>(<span style="color:#007020">cl-defun</span> <span style="color:#963">foo</span> (<span style="color:#038;font-weight:bold">&amp;key</span> (<span style="color:#963">buf</span> (<span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">current-buffer</span>)) (<span style="color:#963">valid-p</span> <span style="color:#036;font-weight:bold">t</span>) (<span style="color:#963">pred</span> <span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">#&#39;</span><span style="color:#963">ignore</span>)
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>                    <span style="color:#038;font-weight:bold">&amp;allow-other-keys</span>)
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>  <span style="color:#888">;; body of foo</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>  )
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>This function has the signature</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#fff;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"><code class="language-emacs-lisp" data-lang="emacs-lisp"><span style="display:flex;"><span>(<span style="color:#963">foo</span> <span style="color:#007020">:buf</span> <span style="color:#963">some-buffer</span> <span style="color:#007020">:valid-p</span> <span style="color:#036;font-weight:bold">t</span> <span style="color:#007020">:pred</span> <span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">#&#39;</span><span style="color:#963">always</span>)
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>which is just a list with the name of the function followed by&hellip; a plist.  So we can build up the plist programmatically and use it directly as <code>foo</code>&rsquo;s argument list:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#fff;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"><code class="language-emacs-lisp" data-lang="emacs-lisp"><span style="display:flex;"><span>(<span style="color:#007020">let</span> ((<span style="color:#963">arglist</span> <span style="color:#333">`</span>(<span style="color:#007020">:buf</span> <span style="color:#333">,</span><span style="color:#963">some-buffer</span> <span style="color:#007020">:valid-p</span> <span style="color:#036;font-weight:bold">t</span> <span style="color:#007020">:pred</span> <span style="color:#333">,</span><span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">#&#39;</span><span style="color:#963">always</span> <span style="color:#007020">:unknown-key</span> <span style="color:#036;font-weight:bold">nil</span>)))
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>  (<span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">apply</span> <span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">#&#39;</span><span style="color:#963">foo</span> <span style="color:#963">arglist</span>))
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>&rsquo;tis a silly thing but oh, so satisfying.  In fact, this is a convenient way to feed a job spec to <code>org-async</code> (<a href="#org-target--org-latex-preview-org-async-explainer">see below</a>).</p>
</div>
</details>
<h4 id="how-extended-info-is-populated">How <code>extended-info</code> is populated</h4>
<p>The setup functions in the <span class="Orange">PREPARE</span> block initialize this bucket with data from parsing the buffer &ndash; i.e., LaTeX fragment locations, equation numbers, etc.  From then on, each task updates <code>extended-info</code> with progressively more information.  The structure of <code>extended-info</code> and this flow of information into it is visualized in this &ldquo;data flow&rdquo; diagram:</p>
<div class="wide">
<figure><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/olp-in-data-flowchart.svg"/>
</figure>

</div>
<p>Contained in &ldquo;Extended Info&rdquo;: &ldquo;Processing Info&rdquo; contains information about and relevant to the external process (LaTeX and the image converter).  &ldquo;Fragment&rdquo; is a list of metadata for fragments to be previewed, including the overlays that will eventually display the preview images.  All of this preview metadata is obtained from the outputs of the latex and image converter runs via the process filters.</p>
<p>Note that Emacs&rsquo; interpreter lock precludes data races and other concurrency issues, since only one function modifies <code>extended-info</code> at a time.</p>
<h4 id="how-extended-info-is-accessed">How <code>extended-info</code> is accessed</h4>
<p>The above chart only captures the flow of data in one direction: from the sources of computation into the <code>extended-info</code> container.  Tasks that don&rsquo;t modify <code>extended-info</code> still need to read it  to do their jobs.  For example,</p>
<ul>
<li><span class="Green">Clean up temporary files</span> needs the paths to the temporary files.</li>
<li><span class="Orange">Cache images and metadata</span> needs the generated image paths, as well as all the geometry metadata.</li>
</ul>
<p>Most functions in the process chain are passed <code>extended-info</code>, or a subset thereof, as an argument.</p>
<figure><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/olp-out-data-flowchart.svg"/>
</figure>

<p>Once again, we get away with it because there are no concurrency problems when there is no concurrency.  This design pattern of passing around a single heap-allocated container across setup functions and callbacks works for all kinds of applications in Emacs.  This is exactly how Org&rsquo;s (synchronous) export system manages information as well.</p>
<h2 id="org-latex-preview-pieces-and-the-org-api"><code>org-latex-preview</code> Pieces and the Org API</h2>
<p>This is where we get into the weeds.  If you&rsquo;re here for the bird&rsquo;s eye view, you can skip ahead to <a href="#live-previews-hooks-galore">Live previews</a> now.</p>
<h3 id="parsing">Parsing</h3>
<p>This is the <span class="Orange">Collect LaTeX Fragments</span> task in the <a href="#figure--org-latex-preview-process-chart">control flow diagram</a>.</p>
<p>Org&rsquo;s syntax is <a href="https://orgmode.org/worg/org-syntax.html#LaTeX_Fragments">extensive</a>, and parsing Org files used to involve a giant mess of regular expressions.  Thankfully the mess is now handled by the <code>org-element</code> library, which provides a simple and powerful API for querying or acting on Org&rsquo;s AST.  A fair amount of work has gone into making it perform well under the constraints of Emacs&rsquo; single-threaded event loop
<span class="sidenote-number"><small class="sidenote">
Besides caching its state, it uses <a href="#the-reactive-ui-hooks-and-timers">timers</a> to schedule parsing work outside of the times the user is interacting with Emacs.
</small></span>
.</p>
<p>Crucially for us, Org has first-class support for LaTeX environments and fragments, and this greatly simplifies parsing and metadata handling.  This support is what makes using LaTeX in Org a pleasant experience in the first place.</p>
<details class="details orgnote" id="org-latex-preview-org-element-explainer">
<summary><span><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/org-mode-icon.png" class=detail-icon></span> The <code>org-element</code> library</summary>
<div class="details">
<p>Added in late 2021, the <code>org-element</code> library greatly simplifies the process of working with Org documents.</p>
<p>I have a minimal working knowledge of the library, so this block covers only the aspects of <code>org-element</code> that we use in <code>org-latex-preview</code>.</p>
<p>Org&rsquo;s syntax distinguishes between &ldquo;elements&rdquo; and &ldquo;objects&rdquo;, where anything &ldquo;larger&rdquo; than a paragraph is an element, and any syntax that could be contained within a paragraph is an object
<span class="sidenote-number"><small class="sidenote">
Incidentally, the default <code>mark-paragraph</code> binding runs <code>org-mark-element</code> in Org mode, which selects the smallest logical element is at point instead.  Try <code>M-h</code> at different locations in an Org buffer to see what element you&rsquo;re on!
</small></span>
.</p>
<p>This distinction matters to us because LaTeX text to be previewed can be either.  As you might expect, inline math (<code>$...$</code> or <code>\(...\)</code>) is an object, and LaTeX environments (<code>\begin{foo}...\end{foo}</code>) are elements, and they differ in the way we must display the preview images.</p>
<p>Every node in the AST as parsed by <code>org-element</code> is made available as a plist whose keys are properties of the element.  These properties include the start and end positions of the element and its contents (<code>:begin</code>, <code>:end</code> and <code>:contents</code>), as well as metadata like properties and keywords.  For example, every element can be named with a <code>#+name:</code> keyword.</p>
<p>These are the only things we need from <code>org-element</code> as far as parsing goes.</p>
</div>
</details>
<p>The objective of the parsing step is to find the bounds of LaTeX fragments in the buffer (or in some region).  In principle, we could do this with <code>org-element</code>:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#fff;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"><code class="language-emacs-lisp" data-lang="emacs-lisp"><span style="display:flex;"><span>(<span style="color:#963">org-element-map</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>    (<span style="color:#963">org-element-parse-buffer</span>)
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>    <span style="color:#333">&#39;</span>(<span style="color:#963">latex-environment</span> <span style="color:#963">latex-fragment</span>)
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>  <span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">#&#39;identity</span>)
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>But this would be too slow, and would allocate far more memory than we need.  Instead, we use a simple regexp scan of the buffer to find the bounds of LaTeX fragments and environments, and <code>org-element</code> only to gather specific metadata about these elements.</p>
<h3 id="preamble-generation">Preamble generation</h3>
<p>This is the <span class="Orange">Create preamble</span> task in the <a href="#figure--org-latex-preview-process-chart">control flow diagram</a>.</p>
<p>Many editor plugins (and web apps) implement LaTeX previews, usually via <a href="https://www.mathjax.org/">MathJax</a> or <a href="https://katex.org/">KaTeX</a>.  KaTeX is fast, and both function well for web pages with limited needs.  But they implement ersatz support for a limited number of LaTeX packages, and cannot consume an actual &ldquo;full-fat&rdquo; LaTeX preamble of the kind you might use when writing a research paper.  There is no <code>\usepackage{}</code> macro or TikZ support, for example.</p>
<p>By comparison, <code>org-latex-preview</code> is backed by a full LaTeX engine and LaTeX distribution, so nothing is off the table.  Of course, this means we need a way to construct the preamble and feed it to the LaTeX engine.</p>
<p>In addition to Emacs-wide settings (like <code>org-latex-preview-preamble</code> and <code>org-latex-packages-alist</code>), document-specific LaTeX headers can be specified with the <code>#+latex_header:</code> directive:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#fff;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"><code class="language-org" data-lang="org"><span style="display:flex;"><span><span style="color:#c00;font-weight:bold">#+latex_header</span><span style="color:#888">: \newcommand{\sOmega}{{\scriptstyle \Omega}}</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span><span style="color:#c00;font-weight:bold">#+latex_header</span><span style="color:#888">: \DeclareMathOperator{\diag}{diag}</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span><span style="color:#c00;font-weight:bold">#+latex_header</span><span style="color:#888">: \DeclareMathOperator{\toep}{toep}</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span><span style="color:#c00;font-weight:bold">#+latex_header</span><span style="color:#888">: \usepackage{calc,cancel}</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span><span style="color:#c00;font-weight:bold">#+latex_header</span><span style="color:#888">: \usepackage{rotating}</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span><span style="color:#c00;font-weight:bold">#+latex_header</span><span style="color:#888">: \usepackage{tikz}</span>
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The combined preamble must be constructed when exporting the Org document to LaTeX, and this machinery lives in the <code>ox-latex</code> library.  <code>org-latex-preview</code> simply calls into it for the preamble.  Among other benefits, this means the preamble used for the preview run is identical to the one used for LaTeX exports
<span class="sidenote-number"><small class="sidenote">
unless you <em>want</em> them to differ, because of course Org provides ways to do that
</small></span>
, so these truly are <em>previews</em>.</p>
<details class="details orgnote">
<summary>The <code>org-export</code> (<code>ox</code>) library <span><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/org-mode-icon.png" class=detail-icon></span></summary>
<div class="details">
<p>The <code>ox</code> library and its children, like <code>ox-latex</code> provide an interface to convert Org documents to any other format, like Pandoc does.</p>
<p>While the specifics (like collating a LaTeX preamble) are output-format dependent, the basic method is uniform and simple.  An exporter maps each Org syntax <a href="#org76ac7ab">element and object</a> to a function that creates the equivalent in the output format, like image links to HTML <code>img</code> tags, or <code>#+begin_quote ... #+end_quote</code> blocks to HTML blockquotes elements.  The fact that the Org syntax supports LaTeX environments natively helps with LaTeX exports.</p>
<p>Of course, when a clean mapping like this is not possible, you can work on the buffer directly and rip out and replace raw text.  Emacs, huh.</p>
</div>
</details>
<p>This does make <code>ox-latex</code> one of the few dependencies of <code>org-latex-preview</code>, which is regrettable given the larger aim of <a href="#latex-previews-everywhere-in-emacs">providing LaTeX previews across Emacs</a>.</p>
<h3 id="precompilation">Precompilation</h3>
<p>This is the <span class="Orange">Precompile LaTeX preamble</span> task in the <a href="#figure--org-latex-preview-process-chart">control flow diagram</a>.</p>
<p>To process a preamble, the LaTeX engine needs to tokenize and execute tens of thousands of lines of macro source.  For a complex preamble, the kind you would use for a book, this can take several seconds on a modern processor.</p>
<p>Since <code>org-latex-preview</code> aims to provide close to real-time feedback as you type, we resort to a trick from the 1990s: we serialize the entire engine state &ndash; the string pool, macro bodies as token lists, font data, engine execution parameters and so on &ndash; into a single binary memory image.  This process, called <em>format dumping</em> or <em>precompilation</em>, was automated for document preambles by David Carlisle&rsquo;s <code>mylatex.ltx</code>, modernized as the <code>mylatexformat</code> package by Florent Chervet.</p>
<p>The upshot is that precompilation saves a significant amount of LaTeX startup time, reducing and (almost) flattening it across all preamble sizes.</p>
<p>Precompilation relies on a number of hacks and only works because pdftex has changed little for decades.  Unfortunately, precompilation is not supported at all by xelatex and only partially by lualatex
<span class="sidenote-number"><small class="sidenote">
preambles that load OpenType fonts or Lua code are unsupported
</small></span>
, so <code>org-latex-preview</code> is slow with the Unicode engines.</p>
<p>Under a <a href="#how-a-modern-latex-preview-system-should-work">different LaTeX preview architecture</a>, none of this would be needed.  But it&rsquo;s what we have so far: a very 90s solution for a very 90s editor.</p>
<h3 id="creating-the-tex-file">Creating the TeX file</h3>
<p>And speaking of being stuck with an old architecture, depending on pdflatex implies that we have to create and feed it a TeX file for each batch of previews
<span class="sidenote-number"><small class="sidenote">
pdflatex and friends do accept input via STDIN, but this did not work out for finicky reasons.
</small></span>
.</p>
<p>This is the <span class="Orange">Create TeX file</span> task in the <a href="#figure--org-latex-preview-process-chart">control flow diagram</a>.</p>
<p>As you might expect, the generated TeX file contains a reference to the precompiled dump file and a listing of all the LaTeX fragments to be previewed.  Instead of placing them into the document separated by something like <code>\pagebreak</code>, we place them inside <code>\begin{preview}</code> and <code>\end{preview}</code> blocks provided by the <a href="https://www.ctan.org/pkg/preview">preview.sty</a> CTAN package.</p>
<p><code>preview.sty</code> is authored by David Kastrup, who also wrote the Emacs-side <code>preview.el</code> package that&rsquo;s part of AUCTeX.  <code>preview.el</code> was the inspiration for <code>org-latex-preview</code> &ndash; it was clear at least that previews in Org mode could be much, much faster.</p>
<p><code>preview.sty</code> does three critical things for us:</p>
<ol>
<li>It places each preview snippet (enclosed within <code>\begin{preview}</code> and <code>\end{preview}</code> blocks) on a separate page in the LaTeX output;</li>
<li>it finds the boundaries of the generated text and crops the page down to them;</li>
<li>and it reports the font size, image size and baseline position in the LaTeX process&rsquo; output stream.</li>
</ol>
<p>These modifications were technically doable through a combination of other packages (Org&rsquo;s old implementation used the tightpage option of the preview machinery), but in cumbersome and error-prone ways.</p>
<p>This makes <code>preview.sty</code> an external dependency of <code>org-latex-preview</code>.  Fortunately this package is included by default even with minimal installs of all common LaTeX distributions.</p>
<h3 id="the-process-runner">The process runner</h3>
<p>The pipeline from the LaTeX fragment text in the TeX file to images suitable for preview consists of two steps.  A LaTeX engine (pdflatex, xelatex or lualatex) produces DVI, XDV or PDF output, which is fed to an image converter (dvisvgm, dvipng or imagemagick) to produce PNG or SVG images.  This is the <span class="Purple">RUN</span> block of the <a href="#figure--org-latex-preview-process-chart">control flow diagram</a>.</p>
<p>This involves a fair bit of bookkeeping, and Emacs&rsquo; callback-based async process management (via &ldquo;process sentinels&rdquo; and &ldquo;process filters&rdquo;) isn&rsquo;t the most ergonomic tool for this.  In the absence of reliable async-io support, we use <code>org-async</code>, a declarative process chain manager now bundled with Org mode.</p>
<p><span class="org-target" id="org-target--org-latex-preview-org-async-explainer"></span></p>
<details class="details orgnote">
<summary>The <code>org-async</code> library <span><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/org-mode-icon.png" class=detail-icon></span></summary>
<div class="details">
<p>Because we need to run a chain of processes with different callback behaviors depending on the exit statuses, <code>org-latex-preview</code> uses <code>org-async</code>, a process queue manager that wraps Emacs&rsquo; <code>start-process</code>, process filter and process sentinel API.  It handles a lot of the bookkeeping for us, providing for</p>
<ul>
<li>declarative specification of a tree of processes that branch on the exit status of a process,</li>
<li>including the ability to register multiple callbacks as required,</li>
<li>keeping track of the context (the <code>extended-info</code> bucket in our case) and passing it to the filter and sentinel,</li>
<li>and limiting the pool of concurrent subprocesses and handling timeouts.</li>
</ul>
<p>The global variable that keeps track of the context data between async calls is managed by <code>org-async</code>.</p>
<hr>
<div class="warning">
<p>The following code might be out of date, please refer to the Org manual.</p>
</div>
<p>An example of an <code>org-async</code> job spec might look like the following (simplified for clarity):</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#fff;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"><code class="language-emacs-lisp" data-lang="emacs-lisp"><span style="display:flex;"><span>(<span style="color:#007020">setq</span> <span style="color:#963">task-spec</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>      (<span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">list</span> <span style="color:#a60;background-color:#fff0f0">&#39;org-async-task</span>             <span style="color:#888">; &lt;-- LaTeX compilation task</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>            <span style="color:#333">&#39;</span>(<span style="background-color:#fff0f0">&#34;latex&#34;</span> <span style="background-color:#fff0f0">&#34;-interaction&#34;</span> <span style="background-color:#fff0f0">&#34;nonstopmode&#34;</span> <span style="background-color:#fff0f0">&#34;texfile.tex&#34;</span>)
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>            <span style="color:#007020">:buffer</span> <span style="color:#036;font-weight:bold">t</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>            <span style="color:#007020">:info</span> <span style="color:#963">extended-info</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>            <span style="color:#007020">:filter</span> <span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">#&#39;</span><span style="color:#963">latex-preview-filter</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>            <span style="color:#007020">:failure</span> <span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">#&#39;</span><span style="color:#963">latex-failure-callback</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>            <span style="color:#007020">:success</span> (<span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">list</span> <span style="color:#a60;background-color:#fff0f0">&#39;org-async-task</span> <span style="color:#888">; &lt;-- Image conversion task</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>                           <span style="color:#333">&#39;</span>(<span style="background-color:#fff0f0">&#34;dvisvgm&#34;</span> <span style="background-color:#fff0f0">&#34;--page=1-&#34;</span> <span style="background-color:#fff0f0">&#34;-o out-%p.svg&#34;</span> <span style="background-color:#fff0f0">&#34;texfile.dvi&#34;</span>)
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>                           <span style="color:#007020">:buffer</span> <span style="color:#036;font-weight:bold">t</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>                           <span style="color:#007020">:info</span> <span style="color:#963">extended-info</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>                           <span style="color:#007020">:filter</span> <span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">#&#39;</span><span style="color:#963">dvisvgm-place-previews-filter</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>                           <span style="color:#007020">:failure</span> (<span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">list</span> <span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">#&#39;</span><span style="color:#963">dvisvgm-failure-callback</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>                                          <span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">#&#39;</span><span style="color:#963">log-errors-callback</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>                                          <span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">#&#39;</span><span style="color:#963">cleanup-callback</span>)
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>                           <span style="color:#007020">:success</span> (<span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">list</span> <span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">#&#39;</span><span style="color:#963">check-fragments</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>                                          <span style="color:#06b;font-weight:bold">#&#39;</span><span style="color:#963">cleanup-callabck</span>))))
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Starting this process chain is simple:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#fff;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"><code class="language-emacs-lisp" data-lang="emacs-lisp"><span style="display:flex;"><span>(<span style="color:#963">org-async-call</span> <span style="color:#963">task-spec</span>)
</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>org-async-call</code> also provides other conveniences like timeouts, retries and concurrent subprocess limits.</p>
</div>
</details>
<p><span class="org-target" id="org-target--org-latex-preview-dvipng-parallel-path"></span>
An example of this bookkeeping is that the pipeline isn&rsquo;t always serial.  When generating PNG images with dvipng, we start LaTeX and dvipng in parallel.  Dvipng processes pages of the DVI file as they appear, while the LaTeX process is still churning them out.  (Generating PNG preview images for a large Org buffer is much faster than the other routes.)</p>
<h3 id="tracking-with-process-filters">Tracking with process filters</h3>
<p>On the other side of the process invocation are the &ldquo;process filters&rdquo;, which are callbacks <a href="#figure--emacs-event-loop-simple">called by the event loop</a> whenever Emacs reads chunks of subprocess output.  This is the <span class="Green">PROCESS FILTERS</span> block in the <a href="#figure--org-latex-preview-process-chart">control flow diagram</a>.</p>
<details class="details apinote">
<summary>A shell metaphor for process filters <span><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/emacs-icon.png" class=detail-icon></span></summary>
<div class="details">
<p>Following the <a href="#org-target--org-latex-preview-process-filters-sentinels">shell metaphor for process filters</a>, a simple way to think of a process filter for an Emacs subprocess is as a sink at the end of a Unix pipe:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#fff;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"><code class="language-sh" data-lang="sh"><span style="display:flex;"><span>some-process 2&gt;&amp;<span style="color:#60e;font-weight:bold">1</span> | process-filter
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>In the LaTeX preview case, the picture is a little more complex as there are multiple processes:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#fff;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"><code class="language-sh" data-lang="sh"><span style="display:flex;"><span>latex -interaction nonstopmode -output-directory /tmp preview-file.tex | latex-process-filter; <span style="color:#666;background-color:#fff0f0;font-weight:bold">\
</span></span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span><span style="color:#666;background-color:#fff0f0;font-weight:bold"></span>dvisvgm --page<span style="color:#333">=</span>1- --bbox<span style="color:#333">=</span>preview -o image-file.svg preview-file.dvi | image-converter-process-filter
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The analogy is different for each of the image converters.  Since <a href="#org-target--org-latex-preview-dvipng-parallel-path">dvipng runs in parallel with LaTeX</a>, for example:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#fff;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"><code class="language-sh" data-lang="sh"><span style="display:flex;"><span><span style="color:#333">{</span> latex -interaction nonstopmode -output-directory /tmp preview-file.tex | latex-process-filter; <span style="color:#333">}</span> &amp; <span style="color:#666;background-color:#fff0f0;font-weight:bold">\
</span></span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span><span style="color:#666;background-color:#fff0f0;font-weight:bold"></span><span style="color:#333">{</span> dvipng --follow -T tight --depth --height -o image-%09d.png preview-file.dvi | image-converter-process-filter; <span style="color:#333">}</span>
</span></span></code></pre></div></div>
</details>
<p>In <code>org-latex-preview</code>&rsquo;s process filters we look for the output (DVI or image) file paths, but also a variety of metadata that preview.sty and dvisvgm emit.  This includes geometry information like the width, height and baseline alignment of the image, and any compilation errors.</p>
