<p><span class="org-target" id="org-target--org-latex-preview-fiddly-details"></span></p>
<details class="details apinote">
<summary>Fine-tuning Emacs&rsquo; process interaction parameters</summary>
<div class="details">
<blockquote>
<p>&ldquo;Whenever Emacs reads chunks of subprocess output&hellip;&rdquo;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This phrasing omits some important but fiddly details about how often and how much output is read at a time, in much the same way as the Unix pipe <code>|</code> symbol does.</p>
<p>While the event loop is switching between <a href="#figure--emacs-event-loop-simple">its four main tasks</a> continuously, there  are (only) a couple of knobs available at the Elisp level to manage how Emacs reads subprocess output.</p>
<ul>
<li><code>read-process-output-max</code> limits how much it reads in one event loop turn.  Setting it to a high value means output from chatty processes will be read sooner, but at the expense of blocking user input.  This is, essentially, Emacs&rsquo; <a href="#figure--emacs-latency-vs-throughput">latency vs throughput</a> curse in a nutshell.</li>
<li><code>process-adaptive-read-buffering</code> tries to delay reading from subprocesses that produce very small chunks of data at a time, as this is inefficient.  In practice turning this on has tended to make things worse instead of better when running most subprocesses.</li>
</ul>
<p>Trying to optimize this for <code>org-latex-preview</code> has proved to be&hellip; difficult.  The rates and sizes of output emitted varies significantly between different LaTeX engines and image converters:</p>
