<p><code>org-latex-preview</code> settles for a broadly acceptable set of defaults in this regard.</p>
</div>
</details>
<p>But the main reason we use process filters here is that we can place images in the buffer as they are generated (and read in from the process STDOUT by the event loop), and don&rsquo;t have to wait for the LaTeX or image converter processes to end.</p>
<h3 id="cleaning-up-with-process-sentinels">Cleaning up with process sentinels</h3>
<p>The unfortunate result of being forced to do file I/O at each step of the pipeline is that we are left with a trail of intermediate files that need to be swept away
<span class="sidenote-number"><small class="sidenote">
Instead of, say, a block of shared memory that can simply be freed.
</small></span>
.  This is the <span class="Orange">POSTPROCESS</span> block in the <a href="#figure--org-latex-preview-process-chart">control flow diagram</a>, accomplished in the &ldquo;process sentinels&rdquo;.</p>
<p>A process sentinel is a callback invoked by the event loop when the process changes status.  In principle this includes any status change (exit, signal, stop/continue), but in practice we only care about it after the process exits.</p>
<p>Clearing the intermediate detritus of the compilation is not an urgent task, so <code>org-latex-preview</code>&rsquo;s process sentinels actually schedule this on an idle timer.  Their other major function in this design is to diagnose process failures and restart the job when the cause looks transient.</p>
<h3 id="caching">Caching</h3>
<p>This is the <span class="Orange">Cache images and metadata</span> task in the <a href="#figure--org-latex-preview-process-chart">control flow diagram</a>.</p>
<p>LaTeX preview generation is an order of magnitude (or two) faster than what Org did before, so caching preview images might seem like a secondary concern.  But <code>org-latex-preview</code> still caches images, for a few reasons:</p>
<ul>
<li>The same state may be encountered dozens of times when editing a fragment with live previews.</li>
<li>We would like to avoid rerunning LaTeX in contexts where the Org document itself is the final output, such as in-Emacs slideshow programs.</li>
<li>When exporting to other formats (HTML or ODT), <code>org-latex-preview</code> is responsible for generating the images to be bundled with the web page or document.</li>
</ul>
<p>In addition to the text of the LaTeX fragment itself, preview images are specific to the LaTeX preview pipeline configuration, the preamble, the Emacs theme and even the fragment&rsquo;s position in the buffer when equation numbering is turned on.  To cache previews reproducibly, all of these inputs are hashed together as the key to the cached image.</p>
<details class="details orgnote">
<summary><span><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/org-mode-icon.png" class=detail-icon></span> The <code>org-persist</code> library</summary>
<div class="details">
<p>While you can specify where the preview images should be cached relative to the document, <code>org-latex-preview</code> defaults to using <code>org-persist</code>, a general-purpose caching library that is part of Org mode.  <code>org-persist</code> is used for various caching duties by Org mode, such as resources fetched from the Internet and the partial syntax trees of parsed Org buffers.</p>
<p>Caching LaTeX preview images actually involves more than copying a file somewhere and serializing a hash table to disk.  We also need to store, as Elisp data, the image display geometry information, including the baseline alignment height.  The <code>org-persist</code> API supports caching different container kinds for exactly this purpose.</p>
<p>Finally, with sufficient time (or a large enough corpus) the cache of LaTeX images can grow to gigabytes in size, so cache entries are set to expire after a month, another function served by <code>org-persist</code>&rsquo;s built-in garbage collection.</p>
</div>
</details>
<h3 id="displaying-images-and-overlay-management">Displaying images and overlay management</h3>
<p>This covers the in-buffer UI and affordances of <code>org-latex-preview</code>, indicated by the <span class="Orange">Create overlays</span> and <span class="Orange">Place LaTeX previews</span> blocks in the <a href="#figure--org-latex-preview-process-chart">control flow diagram</a>.</p>
<p>Beyond what is covered in <a href="#the-frontend-displaying-images-over-text">displaying images over text</a>, displaying the image itself is simple: the image just needs to be resized and aligned according to the recorded geometry.  The overlays themselves are set up before starting the LaTeX run, and a series of overlay properties are set or unset over the course of the process.</p>
<ul>
<li>If an existing image is being updated, the overlay optionally gets a &ldquo;face&rdquo; property indicating that the preview is out of date.</li>
<li>If this is a fresh preview, an overlay property marks it as &ldquo;processing&rdquo;, displayed in the fringe.</li>
<li>If any compilation errors are captured, they&rsquo;re placed as tooltip text via an overlay property.  Via the SVG&rsquo;s <code>currentColor</code> attribute, we also color the overlay image red to indicate that there was an error.</li>
</ul>
<p>These are basic but (hopefully) intuitive indicators of the state of the preview.</p>
<h2 id="live-previews-hooks-galore">Live previews: Hooks galore</h2>
<p>The breakdown provided so far does not explain the claims we made in the objectives section, like real-time (or at least fast) feedback as you type.  As seen in the demos above, there is one more element to making the previews appear &ldquo;live&rdquo; and reactive to cursor movement or buffer edits.</p>
<p>For the seamless hide/show behavior:</p>
<ul>
<li>Moving the cursor into the region covered by the overlay should &ldquo;open&rdquo; the overlay, reveal the underlying text and move the image next to or below the LaTeX fragment.</li>
<li>Moving the cursor out of the region should do the reverse, and &ldquo;close up&rdquo; the overlay.</li>
</ul>
<p>For real-time feedback:</p>
<ul>
<li>Editing a fragment with a visible preview marks the preview out of date, so the image should either disappear or be updated.</li>
<li>If editing displaymath and the numbering changes, all other numbered environments in the buffer should be updated as well.</li>
<li>Editing a previewed fragment in another buffer (with <code>org-edit-special</code>, <code>C-c '</code>) should cause the preview to be updated as you type.</li>
<li>When typing or pasting text, LaTeX environments need to be detected and parsed, and the preview pipeline started in real time.</li>
</ul>
<p>All of these are achieved using hooks, which is ultimately the only mechanism we have to trigger reactive behavior in Emacs.  The hide/show behavior involves tracking the cursor position via <code>pre-command-hook</code> and <code>post-command-hook</code> (made buffer-local).  Buffer edits are tracked via <code>after-change-functions</code>, but with several fast-fail heuristics to avoid slowing down Emacs
<span class="sidenote-number"><small class="sidenote">
The difficulty of identifying, in real time, <code>$</code> and <code>$$</code> as LaTeX delimiter starts, delimiter ends, or as actual dollar signs is why live previews require the unambiguous <code>\(...\)</code> and <code>\[...\]</code> delimiters, even though the dollars are supported in non-live usage.
</small></span>
.  The list goes on, since we need to track Org&rsquo;s folding states, as well as changes in other buffers when using <code>org-edit-special</code>.</p>
<p>The other strategy we&rsquo;re forced to employ, given the event loop&rsquo;s limitations, is to throttle and debounce pipeline calls, batching edits and avoiding race conditions between in-flight LaTeX runs &ndash; the interpreter lock protects Elisp state, but independent subprocesses will happily interleave and clobber each other&rsquo;s output.</p>
<p>Finally, this feature is the source of dozens of edge cases, such as editing fragments inside folded regions (during global text replacement, say), or the many invalid intermediate states of a LaTeX fragment causing the preview to fail repeatedly.  Knocking down these bugs while keeping this path fast was a substantial portion of the effort over the years this library was developed
<span class="sidenote-number"><small class="sidenote">
Now that it&rsquo;s part of Org mode and seeing more widespread use, there are no doubt several more edge cases waiting to be reported!
</small></span>
.</p>
<h2 id="latex-previews-everywhere-in-emacs">LaTeX previews everywhere in Emacs</h2>
<p>A proof of concept: the design of <code>org-latex-preview</code> makes it possible to preview LaTeX outside of Org mode.  Here&rsquo;s an example previewing LaTeX-formatted comments in a prog buffer (Clojure, here):</p>
