<hr>
<p>While this feature exists only as a prototype, it&rsquo;s simple to implement and extend.  To see why, let&rsquo;s look again at the <a href="#figure--org-latex-preview-process-chart">preview process breakdown</a>.  All tasks here are agnostic of Org mode save for two:</p>
<figure><img src="https://karthinks.com/img/olp-non-org-flowchart.svg"/>
</figure>

<ul>
<li><span class="Orange">Collect LaTeX fragments</span>: currently handled by <code>org-element</code>,</li>
<li><span class="Orange">Create preamble</span>: currently assembled by the <code>org-export</code> (<code>ox-latex</code>) library.</li>
</ul>
<p>To use LaTeX previews outside of Org mode, we only need providers for these two tasks:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>A parser that can identify the boundaries of LaTeX fragments in the buffer
<span class="sidenote-number"><small class="sidenote">
Technically you could reuse <code>org-element</code> in non-Org buffers for this, but there will be many false negatives and occasional false positives.
</small></span>
.  This does not need to be a full-fledged parser &ndash; just a regexp scan of the buffer looking for <code>\begin{</code> or <code>\[</code> can suffice.  This is how the above demos were created.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>A LaTeX preamble generator appropriate for the buffer.  This can just be a constant string (instead of a function) that includes a reasonable set of LaTeX packages.  I used Org&rsquo;s default LaTeX preamble string everywhere in the above demos.</p>
</li>
</ul>
