<p>The positions in <code>entries</code> would be obtained from your parser for the major mode.  For <code>preamble</code> you can just reuse or tweak <code>org-latex-preview-preamble</code> in a pinch.  See the function documentation for more details.</p>
<p>Requiring the <code>org-latex-preview</code> library will in turn load a few Org libraries (<code>org-element</code>, <code>org-persist</code>, <code>ox</code> and <code>org-macs</code>), but it does <em>not</em> load all of Org.  We tried hard to keep from loading the 16,000-line behemoth that is <code>org.el</code>.</p>
</div>
</details>
<p>As of late 2026, abstracting the library from its major-mode adapters remains on the TODO list.</p>
<h2 id="how-a-modern-latex-preview-system-should-work">How a modern LaTeX preview system <em>should</em> work</h2>
<p>When we started <code>org-latex-preview</code> in 2021, live-updating previews were not available anywhere except one LaTeX formula-editing website and, to a lesser degree, <a href="https://www.lyx.org/">LyX</a>.  We often wondered why, since <code>preview.sty</code> did most of the work for you and the editor side was pretty simple to implement.  If <code>org-latex-preview</code> were a thrown-together external Emacs package providing many of the same features (sans polish), it could have been written over a couple of weekends.  I know this because <a href="https://github.com/karthink/org-preview">I wrote it</a>.  <a href="https://github.com/karthink/org-auctex">Twice</a>.</p>
<p>Over the years, however, this feature has grown in ubiquity and there are plugins for most note-taking (Obsidian) and editing software (Neovim, VS Code) that provide this.  Emacs itself has at least four active implementations of live LaTeX previews now, and a frontier LLM can probably implement a basic version for your preferred platform with one prompt.</p>
<p>Even so, Emacs remains the oldest platform to implement this feature, and does so in the most complete and mature form
<span class="sidenote-number"><small class="sidenote">
As of 2026.
</small></span>
.  Considering I spent a lot of this write-up complaining about the limitations of the Elisp runtime, this is remarkable.</p>
<p>But creating temporary files after every buffer change and spawning separate LaTeX processes remains a terrible way to do real-time updates.  Precompilation is another hack, unsupported by LaTeX engines besides pdflatex.  While <code>org-latex-preview</code> is a massive improvement over the previous LaTeX preview implementation for Org mode, it&rsquo;s still the Elisp equivalent of a Bash script that got out of hand.  Quite simply, this is the wrong level at which to address the problem.  A better way to implement continuous previews would be to talk to a managed process over a pipe or socket.  The right approach here is to work at the level of the TeX engine, as <a href="https://github.com/let-def/texpresso">TeXPresso</a> does.</p>
<p><span class="org-target" id="org-target--org-latex-preview-dynamic-modules"></span>
But it would be even better to avoid inter-process communication entirely and use an <a href="https://www.phimulambda.org/blog/emacs-dynamic-module.html">Emacs dynamic module</a>, linking with a modern LaTeX library that does incremental (instead of batch) compilation.  &ldquo;Lightweight&rdquo; alternatives like KaTeX or MathJaX don&rsquo;t cut it, since we need a full-fat LaTeX compiler, macros and all.  One possible candidate for this is <a href="https://github.com/HenryXiaoYang/realtime-tex">realtime-tex</a>, the <code>rtex</code> library, which embeds LuaLaTeX (the most modern of the LaTeX engines) and compiles incrementally and effectively instantaneously
<span class="sidenote-number"><small class="sidenote">
Although I would be inclined to wait for the project to mature.
</small></span>
.  This would be a quality improvement almost as drastic as the one <code>org-latex-preview</code> makes over its predecessor.</p>
<p>Of course, a dynamic module linking an incremental compiler is a completely different architecture from what we have now, and would share at most the image display and UI layer.  So projects like this and the inevitable official Typst preview package for Org are tabled for another day.  Maybe you&rsquo;ll be the one to implement them?</p>
